#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f1a6b8-bc2c-5a80-8357-295783024abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387511} {:answer/id #uuid "a57e6895-e059-55cc-80d4-076bda8bbcbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387512} {:answer/id #uuid "a4864502-3f35-5e7f-a3e1-d0df7a16fa68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387513} {:answer/id #uuid "573d87f6-891c-57dd-9c28-e62de3e6efc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387514} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a1377f-df64-552e-baf8-948fbafe555e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a57e6895-e059-55cc-80d4-076bda8bbcbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387512} :answer-field/id #uuid "f07897ba-d1f0-5e68-8ef8-cd7504c064ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c70dbc22-a4b5-5480-a11f-1031f28c8756" :question/math-academy-id "q-122964" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/0fefe892c74e8d391e56ee822f83e7e0fe0110593c9d6a4b93b8cfe1e558ab16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/fe872f1f1b0307760dcdba6ac6f6be20f197662ac250ab46dc77fa472295906f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=7 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=7+4+4+1=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ad56ee-c6ac-577b-95cd-f34e1771756d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387518} {:answer/id #uuid "9b155ead-8af5-52bb-b827-a826c304754b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387519} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc329a9-0a6f-5898-aae7-f58335069690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387520} {:answer/id #uuid "1885cad9-36a6-5857-953f-386cc5133120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387521} {:answer/id #uuid "2caf64d0-108a-5934-825d-81a5c9eb0949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2caf64d0-108a-5934-825d-81a5c9eb0949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387522} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f76a934-c144-5429-b6ca-5c77d902fb9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2917fbcf-2187-5853-8927-eace7c8bfe2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122982" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,4]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3,4},\\,{P}_{2}={0,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/c30acb7cb94886e612675d5e64838961134e8d09e5d88c1d803067832f4b173e.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{31}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+{y}^{2}+2$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/e5c92768512480b16fcca735126b303c4ca372d22a5c735fc8cea1588a3c5811.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/b015f94b135ca669c0711057c7245982eb8f0257e4947a603571f2826364b8fa.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,0)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,0)=4 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,0)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76a6d6cf-e8fe-50ad-b54c-5443b4566197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387525} {:answer/id #uuid "51c2f462-62c4-5dc7-ae5e-7e7de58aec52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387526} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d8dcbf-db25-58f9-83fb-5b70fca925d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387527} {:answer/id #uuid "927811f7-d6e8-55cb-b530-b216a6039fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387528} {:answer/id #uuid "c836c950-1848-54ba-b6c0-ab0d5418b753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1d8dcbf-db25-58f9-83fb-5b70fca925d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387527} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0f2d9b-2f51-5718-bad4-a126527a1a3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "135c39c5-8376-539f-a46b-90fbc404bfa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75238" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/193d8dc290095a750fe6c7fb6a5d6e75efb342e213f8aabc8fb7921cd335242e.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/a8c9191c43ca304fee01dbb4298c5312f1993ebd25aef266dd9a4828af5f25d5.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =1+4+4+7 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d9a8d6-b403-569f-9a95-8044750dcfce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387532} {:answer/id #uuid "31e60bfd-a28e-52e4-902c-a697474adc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387533} {:answer/id #uuid "ce92c15b-ad1c-5b12-92dc-3eeb3d9c1eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387534} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b7d59c-7836-5633-bc22-452bd79702fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387535} {:answer/id #uuid "1b87263a-2a28-5299-92ad-a550a47ab105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05d9a8d6-b403-569f-9a95-8044750dcfce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387532} :answer-field/id #uuid "10013bee-bffd-5561-99b3-880256fe1e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d52aff52-1e67-52b2-affa-9c2158c1c6ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-75249" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [2,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2x+y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/01edff0feb1bf7545526c3eb258ffb608a982fae094d83a0512b006ec930a60f.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/335148bb25604ed918640c6a21feb7de3ede3a2cd340fbc5288442bbe3ed3d6d.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/b0c98c2e3785d1b0ccdd8675f89f6a83372dfc1f1ca60c76c638871d56add91b.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,2)=6 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,3)=5 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,3)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =4+6+5+7 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e485936-79cd-51b3-8faa-dbb36a81f646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387539} {:answer/id #uuid "032f704d-bbd8-51f2-9675-cf7e8147a4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387540} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4d9309-edc7-5aac-b953-8f68f3d1351f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387541} {:answer/id #uuid "121ce215-db40-56ca-a288-74cfcc9f7a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387542} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d05d33-8e8f-53d4-8fc9-11c7756540ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e485936-79cd-51b3-8faa-dbb36a81f646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387539} :answer-field/id #uuid "38ab7d44-8beb-5061-ad5c-dce6e5437b37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7401229-63d2-5b7e-9354-110723dd0a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-75254" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/0fefe892c74e8d391e56ee822f83e7e0fe0110593c9d6a4b93b8cfe1e558ab16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083084/assets/fe872f1f1b0307760dcdba6ac6f6be20f197662ac250ab46dc77fa472295906f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=6 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=6+3+3+0=12.\n$$"}]}
